      "weight_variable_name": null,
      "weight_fields_interpretation": "The study uses PISA 2018 microdata (612,004 respondents from 79 countries) and applies a supervised classification pipeline to predict academic performance categories derived from PISA plausible values. The manuscript describes data cleaning (list deletion when missingness >50%, otherwise imputation using mode/mean/median/random draws) and feature selection, but it does not report using PISA sampling weights or any replicate-weight variance estimation. Because the models are implemented in RapidMiner and evaluated via a random 80/20 split, the analysis appears to be conducted without complex survey weighting, which may affect population-representativeness and uncertainty quantification under PISA’s clustered design (students within schools within countries). No weight variable name (e.g., W_FSTUWT/TOTWGT) or replicate method (BRR/jackknife) is provided, so weighting cannot be verified."
    },
    "plausible_values_handling": "rubin_rules",
    "missing_data_handling": "multiple_imputation",
    "handling_not_reported_explanation": null,
    "sample_details": {
      "total_students": 612004,
      "countries": [],
      "sample_filtering_criteria": "Uses PISA 2018 eligible student population aged 15 years 3 months to 16 years 2 months, enrolled in grade 7 or higher, with exclusions at school level (geographically inaccessible schools and special needs schools) and at student level (students with intellectual/functional disabilities and students with limited language proficiency). Includes both computer-based and paper-based tests and uses the full available PISA respondent set after these eligibility/exclusion steps; no additional country-specific subsampling is described."
    },
